        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.602,
            0.042,
            0.848,
            0.058
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Atomic Foundations of Matter"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 167"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.088,
            0.616,
            0.191
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the above activities, we have used many compounds. Let us learn how these are formed from the elements. Let us take an example of water, is there a fixed ratio in which hydrogen can combine with oxygen to form it, or can they combine in any ratio? This leads us to another important law."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.203,
            0.525,
            0.224
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9.2 Law of Constant Proportions"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.227,
            0.616,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Soon after Lavoisier, Joseph Proust proposed that in any compound formed by two or more elements, the elements combine in a fixed ratio by mass. In other words, elements in a compound have definite proportions irrespective of its source. For example, if the water collected from various sources, such as rivers, borewells or the ocean, is purified and analysed. It is always found to contain hydrogen and oxygen in a mass ratio of 1:8. What does it convey? If 9 g of purified water from any source is decomposed, 1 g of hydrogen and 8 g of oxygen are always obtained. This proportion is written as 1:8."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.416,
            0.615,
            0.467
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This is known as the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Law of Constant Proportions",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", or the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Law of Definite Proportions",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", or sometimes as "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Proust’s Law",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.662,
            0.079,
            0.828,
            0.095
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meet a Scientist"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.652,
            0.104,
            0.755,
            0.195
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.652,
                0.104,
                0.755,
                0.195
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_7.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.759,
            0.104,
            0.918,
            0.199
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Joseph Louis Proust",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " was a prominent French chemist known for his careful experimental"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.65,
            0.199,
            0.918,
            0.461
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "work. He contributed to the Law of Definite Proportions by showing that chemical compounds always contain elements in fixed ratios by mass. For example, Proust studied the composition of copper carbonate. He showed that copper carbonate always contains copper, carbon and oxygen in the same proportion by mass, no matter how it was prepared or where it was found. His work laid an important foundation that helped shape modern chemistry."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.474,
            0.204,
            0.507
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.139,
                0.474,
                0.204,
                0.507
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_10.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.212,
            0.482,
            0.428,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.509,
            0.785,
            0.635
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In many ancient civilisations, red pigment derived from rocks was widely used in painting and as a colouring agent for various objects. In India, it was known as hingula, and in Latin and English, as cinnabar. Over the centuries, it was discovered in many civilisations that heating cinnabar could yield two elements — mercury and sulfur in mass percentage of around 86.22% and 13.78%, respectively. Interestingly, most civilisations also found that grinding mercury and sulfur together in this ratio could form cinnabar, although the toxic nature of both prevented this process from becoming widespread."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.792,
            0.513,
            0.905,
            0.591
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 13,
              "bbox": [
                0.792,
                0.513,
                0.905,
                0.591
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_13.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 14,
              "bbox": [
                0.788,
                0.599,
                0.915,
                0.614
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 9.5: Cinnabar"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.653,
            0.775,
            0.705
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 9.3:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Sodium chloride (NaCl) contains sodium and chlorine in the mass ratio of 23:35.5. If 46 g of sodium reacts completely, how much chlorine is needed to form NaCl?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.708,
            0.641,
            0.726
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Solution:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Mass of chlorine required = (35.5 ÷ 23) × 46 = 71 g"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.733,
            0.188,
            0.763
          ],
          "content": [
